# Complex Nanostructures from Materials based on Metal-Organic Frameworks for Electrochemical Energy Storage and Conversion

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention because of their abundant diversity in structure and composition. Recently, there has been growing research interest in deriving advanced nanomaterials with complex architectures and tailored chemical compositions from MOF-based precursors for electrochemical energy storage and conversion. Here, a comprehensive overview of the synthesis and energy-related applications of complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based precursors is provided. After a brief summary of synthetic methods of MOF-based templates and their conversion to desirable nanostructures, delicate designs and preparation of complex architectures from MOFs or their composites are described in detail, including porous structures, single-shelled hollow structures, and multishelled hollow structures, as well as other unusual complex structures. Afterward, their applications are discussed as electrode materials or catalysts for lithium-ion batteries, hybrid supercapacitors, water-splitting devices, and fuel cells. Lastly, the research challenges and possible development directions of complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based-templates for electrochemical energy storage and conversion applications are outlined.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention because of their abundant diversity in structure and composition. Recently, there has been growing research interest in deriving advanced nanomaterials with complex architectures and tailored chemical compositions from MOFbased precursors for electrochemical energy storage and conversion. Here, a comprehensive overview of the synthesis and energy-related applications of complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based precursors is provided. After a brief summary of synthetic methods of MOF-based templates and their conversion to desirable nanostructures, delicate designs and preparation of complex architectures from MOFs or their composites are described in detail, including porous structures, single-shelled hollow structures, and multishelled hollow structures, as well as other unusual complex structures. Afterward, their applications are discussed as electrode materials or catalysts for lithium-ion batteries, hybrid supercapacitors, water-splitting devices, and fuel cells. Lastly, the research challenges and possible development directions of complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based-templates for electrochemical energy storage and conversion applications are outlined.
## 1. Introduction
Since the industrial revolution, huge amount of fossil fuels has been consumed to power the economy and deliver unprecedented affluence to human society. Currently, fossil fuels, such as petroleum, natural gas, and coal, still dominate (above $80\%$ ) the total global energy consumption. Combustion of these fuels inevitably emits massive amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ which is considered as a main greenhouse gas causing global warming. A comprehensive strategy against climate change is through a low-carbon-emission economy by increasing utilization of renewable and environment friendly energy sources and storage devices. As a result, tremendous efforts are being made to advance sustainable energy storage and conversion systems, such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs),[1,2] hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs),[3] water-splitting electrolyzers,[4] and fuel cells.[5] However, low-cost and large-scale application of these technologies demands further improvement of their performance and efficiency.
To promote electrochemical reactions and sustain structural stabilities, nanostructured electrodes or catalysts, particularly in porous and/or hollow forms, have attracted great attention. These materials exhibit many structuredependent advantages, originating from the high surface-to-mass/volume ratio, interparticle pore channel/void, small grain size, and robust secondary architecture.[6–14] Specifically, high surface area provides abundant active sites for surface-related processes, including insertion/ deinsertion, adsorption/desorption, heterogeneous catalysis, and surface redox reactions.[15–19] Large pore volume and open pore structure are critically important for loading guest species, accommodating mechanical strains and facilitating mass transport.[10,20–25] Solid-state diffusion distance in porous and hollow particles (i.e., thickness of wall or shell) is significantly below the size of particles, surmounting the diffusion restriction in nonporous materials.[26–31] The confinement capability of pore channels and cavities also shows great promise for certain applications.[32–39] More importantly, extraordinary size-dependent physical and chemical properties in nanostructured materials are not available in their bulk counterparts.[11,40]
Increasing the complexity of nanostructures in terms of architecture, structural subunit, and chemical composition has drawn tremendous interest for both fundamental studies and practical applications.[11,41–43] Delicate and rational design of complex nanostructures may endow materials with characteristics that are required for many current and emerging applications.[44,45] For electrochemical applications, complex architectures tend to exhibit improved performance compared with simple nanoparticle-assembled structures.[46–48] For instance, complex porous structures constructed by secondary subunits with highly exposed active sites on their surfaces are expected to maximize the electrocatalytic activity.[49–51] Moreover, multi­ shelled hollow particles as charge storage materials can improve the volumetric energy density due to the optimized empty space and relatively high fraction of active component compared with their single-shelled counterparts.[52] Nevertheless, controllable construction of complex nanostructures encounters even greater synthetic challenges.
As an important subgroup of coordination polymers (CPs) featuring crystalline structures and potential porosity, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have become a rapidly expanding research area in the last two decades.[53–56] The most distinctive advantage of MOF materials is designable frameworks formed via a modular self-assembly process, which can incorporate various functional species of metal ions/clusters and organic linkers.[57–59] In addition, nanostructured functional materials derived from MOFs have been playing important roles in energy-related applications.[60–62] In view of the certain thermal and chemical instability, MOFs can be easily converted to inorganic functional materials via pyrolysis under inert atmosphere or chemical reaction with desirable reagents. Depending on the conversion method, MOF precursors could be transformed into carbon, metal-based compounds and their composites with diverse porous or hollow nanostructures.[63–68] In addition to using simple MOF nanoparticles as precursors, delicate design and integration of MOFs and functional nanomaterials into novel multicompositional MOF-based nanocomposites could be used to construct complex nanostructured materials with designable structures and compositions.[67,69,70] These MOF-derived complex nanostructures might exhibit multiple structure- and composition-dependent merits, offering potential solutions to the key challenges in electrochemical applications for sustainable energy.
Herein, we give a comprehensive review of the latest development of complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based materials for electrochemical energy storage and conversion. First, we provide a brief summary of synthetic strategies for MOF-based precursors and their derived nanomaterials. Then, the controllable syntheses of these complex nanostructures are extensively discussed based on their structural features. Specifically, these architectures are categorized into porous structures, single-shelled hollow structures, multishelled hollow structures, and other unusual complex structures with tunable structural subunits and compositions. Afterward, their applications as electrode materials or catalysts for LIBs, HSCs, water splitting electrolyzers, and fuel cells are further discussed. Lastly, we close with a perspective on research challenges and development directions of MOF-derived nanomaterials for energy storage and conversion.
## 2. Synthetic Strategies of MOF-Based Precursors and their Derived Nanomaterials
Synthesizing MOF-derived nanomaterials with well-controlled structures and compositions offers the possibility to study and tailor their electrochemical properties. Figure 1 and Table  1 summarize various methods to synthesize MOF-based precursors and further transform them into functional materials.
