Here, we discuss how to obtain nanostructured materials from MOFs by manipulating: i) the fabrication of MOF-based precursors and ii) the conversion process to the desired functional materials.
Typically, MOF particles with controllable sizes, shapes, and compositions can be obtained by solution-based methods when combining metal ions and organic ligands under appropriate reaction conditions. Afterward, self-pyrolysis under inert atmosphere or chemical reaction with different reagents, such as reactive gases, vapors, and ions in solutions, is typically applied to produce different nanostructured MOF-derived materials. In early studies, conventional MOF particles have been commonly used as precursors. Nevertheless, most recent works demonstrate that nanostructures of MOFs and MOFbased composite precursors would offer new opportunities for synthesis of nanostructured materials with high complexity in terms of both architecture and composition, which will be primarily discussed.
2.1. Strategies for Synthesis of MOF-Based Precursors 2.2. Strategies for Synthesis of MOF-Derived Nanomaterials
Various methods have been demonstrated to generate different MOF-based materials with complex structures and tailored compositions. Based on the formation process of MOF-based precursors, most of these methods can be broadly categorized into seven groups: i) controlled etching, ii) outward diffusion, iii) impregnation with functional species, iv) blending assembly, v) surface/interface growth, vi) surface coating with functional shells, and vii) electrospinning.
The controlled etching method is generally used to create hollow space by selectively dissolving the inner part of solid MOF precursors.[71,72] The as-obtained hollow particles often retain the crystal structure and size of original MOF templates. MOF hollow structures can also be formed by outward diffusion process, where the inner cavity is generated by relocation of metal–organic species to outer region of parent MOF particles.[30,31,73] The structural evolution is often accompanied by particle growth or phase transition of MOF precursors. These two methods are effective in controlling the architecture of pure MOF precursors.
Impregnation of MOF particles with guest species, such as ions or molecules, through a diffusion–deposition process is a classic approach to produce MOF composites. In general, guest species can be introduced into MOF materials in one-pot synthesis or by post-infiltration, which serve as coprecursors to form active components during the subsequent conversion process.[74–87] The MOF matrix offers a limited nanospace to confine the size of the functional moieties and inhibit their agglomeration. However, such an impregnation strategy limits tunability of size, shape, and composition of the nanoparticles incorporated in the MOF matrix. The blending assembly method can overcome these difficulties by homogeneously encapsulating presynthesized particles within MOF particles, typically via cooperative self-assembly of functional nanoparticles, metal ions, and organic ligands in solution.[88–95]
For surface/interface growth processes, nucleation, and growth of MOFs take place around substrate materials, such as particles, oil droplets, or films. Such controllable formation of MOFs on the surface of substrates or at the interface between the substrate and the MOF precursor solution creates diverse MOF-based nanostructures, such as core–shelled composites, colloidosomes, membranes, and arrays.[70,96–98] Alternatively, surface of MOFs can be functionalized with shells of other materials, such as silica, titania, or biopolymer. These core–shelled MOF composites might gain additional functionalities from the coating shells and exhibit improved stability and dispersity compared with their pristine counterparts.[99–102]
In addition, conventional nanofabrication techniques would also benefit the development of MOF-based nanostructures. For example, as a well-developed technique to prepare fiber architectures in polymer engineering, electro­spinning has also been applied to prepare 1D MOF composites.[103–106] This method ensures easy fabrication of various MOF composite fibers with a wide range of MOF– polymer combinations.
In addition to the manipulation of MOF-based precursors, approaches for their conversion to MOF derivatives largely affect the nanostructure and composition of the resulting functional nanomaterials. Due to the inherent porosity and presence of both metal and organic moieties, MOF-based materials are naturally suitable as precursors for a wide variety of metal/ carbon-based nanostructured materials, such as metal compounds, heteroatom-doped carbons, and their composites. The thermal and chemical instability of MOFs, which might hinder their direct applications, enable their controllable conversion by using various methods. Formation of porous or hollow structures with tunable architectures and compositions can be easily achieved using MOF-derived strategies without extra templates or tedious procedures, showing notable advantages over conventional methods. Based on the reaction mechanism during the conversion process, the synthetic methods can be summarized as four categories: i) self-pyrolysis in inert atmosphere, ii) chemical reaction with gases/vapors, iii) chemical reaction with solutions, and iv) postchemical etching.
Self-pyrolysis of MOF-based precursors under inert atmosphere typically leads to metal/carbon-based composites via an in situ carbonization process.[107–116] The resulting hybrid nanomaterials usually exhibit porous structure and preserve the morphology of original MOF particles, and consist of nanosized metal, metal oxide, or metal carbide species in porous carbon frameworks.[67,69,114–116] In the presence of reactive gases or vapors, the solid–gas phase reaction easily converts MOF-based templates into diverse nanostructured materials.[117,118] For instance, hollow structured metal oxides have been prepared by controllable decomposition of MOF precursors at high temperature in air.[45,119–122] Moreover, various metal-based compounds including sulfides,[123,124] phosphides,[125–127] and selenides,[65] can be facilely fabricated via reaction of MOF-based templates with corresponding gases/vapors. Similarly, solid–liquid phase reaction of MOF-based templates with various reactants in solution can produce nanomaterials with tunable structures and compositions as well.[44,66,128–131] For example, ion exchange reactions of MOF-based precursors with ions in solution can easily occur if the coordinating interaction between metal ions and organic ligands is relatively weak in the MOF matrix, leading to the formation of metal-based inorganic compounds.
In addition to these direct conversion methods, post-treatments have been applied to modify the composition and nanostructure. For example, to selectively remove undesirable components from MOF-derived composite materials, postchemical etching is often used.[107,111] Leaching metal/carbon-based composites from selfpyrolysis of MOFs in acid solution has been often adopted to remove the metal-based component and produce porous carbon.
## 3. Controlled Synthesis of Complex MOF-Derived Nanostructures
## 3.1. Porous Structures
MOFs and their composites are suitable precursors for the preparation of porous nanomaterials in view of their inherent high porosity. Porous carbon, metal oxide, or other metal/ carbon-based nanostructures can be derived from MOF-based precursors under different reaction conditions, due to the coexistence of organic, metallic, or other guest species in their frameworks.[61,132,133] Direct pyrolysis of MOFs with or without extra carbon sources under inert atmosphere could easily produce porous carbon,[109,113,134] while complex carbon structures require delicate design of the MOF precursors. As an example, Tang et al. reported the fabrication of a N-doped carbon $@$ highly graphitic carbon (denoted as $\mathsf{N C@G C)}$ core–shelled structure by direct pyrolysis of core–shell structured ZIF $-8@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}67$ (ZIF: zeolite imidazole framework) precursors, followed by an acidic etching treatment to remove metal residue.[135] Figure 2a presents the scheme of the synthetic route of ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ particles. The ZI $\mathrm{F}{-}8@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ core–shelled precursors are initially prepared via surface coating of an epitaxial ZIF-67 shell on the ZIF-8 core, which are iso-structures with similar lattice parameters. After annealing under nitrogen atmosphere and subsequent etching treatment, the as-derived ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ core–shelled particles retain the morphology of their parent MOF precursors and show distorted surfaces (Figure 2b). A closer observation on the shell of the ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ particles reveals abundant layered graphitic carbon structures and distinct mesopores (Figure 2c), hence exhibiting a high Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $1276\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
In view of the hybrid composition, MOFs are ideal precursors for nanocomposites with strong coupling between metaland carbon-based moieties. For instance, Li and co-workers recently demonstrated the formation of a novel $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{N}$ -doped carbon nanocomposites with stable Co single atoms on porous carbon matrix by using $Z\mathrm{n-Co}$ -based MOF as the precursor.[116] During annealing under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere $(800-900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the organic linkers of the MOFs are converted to N-doped porous carbon, meanwhile the metal nodes are reduced at high temperature in the in situ generated reductive environment. The proper control of the annealing temperature and the $Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Co}}$ ratio ensures the formation of Co single atoms. Adding an appropriate amount of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ could promote the replacement of a certain proportion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites and serve as a fence to increase the adjacent distance of Co atoms, thus preventing the formation of ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Co}}$ bonds at high temperature. The low boiling point of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms allows their evaporation at high temperatures above $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , leaving free N-sites during pyrolysis to anchor Co atoms (Figure 2d,e).
Similarly, porous metal/carbon-based composite nanostructures can be derived from MOFs, sometimes with extra precursors.[136,137] For example, Wu et al. demonstrated the synthesis of molybdenum carbide $(\mathrm{MoC}_{x})$ octahedra consisting of small nanocrystals embedded in porous carbon matrix from a Cubased MOF containing Mo source.[67] As shown in Figure 2f, Cu–Mo-based MOF precursor is first prepared via a one-pot reaction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions, organic ligand benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxyl­ate and Mo-based polyoxometalates. After that, the as-prepared MOF composite octahedra are directly pyrolyzed at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen atmosphere to generate $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}\mathrm{-Cu}$ nano­ composites. Finally, the $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ octahedra (Figure $^{2\mathrm{g,h}}$ ) composed of small nanocrystals are produced by selectively etching the Cu species with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ solution. Rather than the commonly formed ${\beta{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Mo}}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ , the crystal structure of the as-derived $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ is hexagonal $\eta{\mathrm{-MoC}}$ , suggesting the unique feature of the carburization mechanism confined in the MOF matrix. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ octahedra possess rich mesopores with a large BET specific surface area of $147{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ .
Very recently, Guan et al. reported a dual-MOFs confinedpyrolysis approach for the preparation of iron carbide $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}\right)$ nanoparticles embedded N-doped carbon nanotube (N-CNT) assemblies supported by a porous N-doped carbon matrix $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{CNT}$ assemblies).[69] Unlike conventional methods starting with a single MOF, the synthetic strategy relies on a composite precursor prepared through a blending assembly process with two distinct MOFs: iron-based MOF (MIL-88) nanorods embedded in the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based ZIF-8 host (Figure 2i). The introduction of the MIL-88 guests into the ZIF-8 host as co-precursors guarantees homogeneously confined pyrolysis of MIL-88 nanorods and facile production of assemblies of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanocrystals-embedded N-CNTs (Figure 2j,l). Additionally, the carbonization reaction confined within the carbon framework can effectively limit the aggregation of in situ produced $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanocrystals (Figure 2k,m). The as-derived $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies possess a porous structure with a BET specific surface area of about $377{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ .
## 3.2. Single-Shelled Hollow Structures
Due to the low density of MOFs, there is usually a huge volume contraction when converting MOFs into inorganic materials, which could be utilized to create hollow metal/carbon-based nanostructures.[132] Most hollow structured MOF derivatives are synthesized through thermal treatments or ion exchange reactions under appropriate conditions. Recently, He et al. reported the synthesis of novel nanoboxes containing Ni–Co phosphides and amorphous carbon $\mathrm{(NiCoP/C}$ nanoboxes) through a phosphidation treatment of ZIF- $67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ layered double hydroxide $(Z\mathrm{IF}{-}67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ LDH) nanoboxes.[125] When Co-based ZIF-67 nanocubes react with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ in ethanol solution, the inner region of ZIF-67 nanocubes are selectively etched to form ZIF-67 nanoboxes by in situ generated acidic environment from the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions. Meanwhile, the released ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions will coprecipitate with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions to form a thin layer of Ni–Co LDH on the surface of the as-formed ZIF-67 nanoboxes. Finally, these $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF-}67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ LDH nanoboxes are chemically transformed to NiCoP/C nanoboxes by annealing treatment with sodium hypophosphite under nitrogen atmosphere at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 3a,b). In another example, Hu et al. reported the novel synthesis of complex nanoboxes with a CoSe-rich inner shell and a carbon-rich outer shell $(\mathrm{CoSe/C}$ nanoboxes).[65] During the annealing treatment with selenium powder, ZIF-67 nanocubes are converted to $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ nanoboxes with homogenous shells. With an additional heating process at a higher temperature, ${\mathrm{CoSe}}_{2}$ nanoparticles are transformed into CoSe nanoparticles accumulated in the inner shells of asformed CoSe/C nanoboxes (Figure 3c,d).
In addition to single-shelled hollow architectures consisting of 0D nanoparticles, hollow nanostructures constructed by 1D or 2D building blocks can also be derived from MOF-based templates. For example, Xia et al. reported successful derivation of N-doped carbon nanotubes frameworks (NCNTFs) via carbonization of
ZIF-67 polyhedral particles in reductive atmosphere.[138] During the pyrolysis process, organic species in ZIF-67 precursors serve as both carbon and nitrogen sources for N-doped carbon nanotubes (CNTs) catalyzed by the in situ formed metallic Co nanoparticles. This unique evolution process consumes the central part of MOF template and generates an inner cavity. As confirmed by both field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observation, the hierarchical NCNTFs are constructed by interconnected CNTs as the subunits (Figure 3e,f). Furthermore, CoS nanoparticles embedded porous carbon polyhedra/carbon nanotubes (CoS $@$ PCP/CNTs) have been successfully prepared through a facile one-step route (Figure 3g).[123] In the proposed approach, ZIF-67 polyhedral precursors are first prepared and then directly pyrolyzed after mixing with sulfur powder at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under argon atmosphere. During the sulfidation reaction, ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions in the MOF matrix are transformed to CoS nanoparticles, meanwhile the organic ligands undergo an in situ carbonization process to form the porous carbon host together with the extruded CNTs on its surface. It is suggested that the growth of CNTs is promoted by sulfur in the presence of a Co-based catalyst during the sulfidation process of ZIF-67 templates.
Unique hierarchical ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes with 2D subunits have been successfully prepared by Zhang and co-workers through oxidative decomposition of Prussian blue (PB) cubes in air.[139] Thermal conversion of PB cubes, which are prepared by a solution-based method, is carried out to prepare ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ nanostructures. When the conversion temperature is raised to $650^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the presence of a large temperature gradient along the radial direction leads to the formation of hierarchical $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ boxes constructed by ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ nano­ plate subunits, as evident from Figure 3h,i. Hu et al. presented an MOF template-engaged synthesis of CoS-nanosheet-assembled single-shelled nanoboxes (CoS-NS SSNBs) through two ion exchange reactions.[130] The as-prepared ZIF-67 nanocubes initially undergo an anion exchange with $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ to form ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}.$ nanosheet-assembled single-shelled nanoboxes $(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{-NS}$ SSNBs) by refluxing in a mixed solvent of ethanol and water. After another anion exchange reaction between ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}–{\mathrm{NS}}$ SSNBs and $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ ions, ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanosheets are further transformed into amorphous CoS while the morphology of the parti­ cles remain intact (Figure 3j). TEM characterization (Figure 3k) clearly reveals that the hollow nanostructures are assembled by nanosheet building blocks. Furthermore, incorporation of multiple metals could be realized by controlling the conversion reaction. By reacting Ni–Co Prussian blue analogue (PBA) nanocubes with ammonium thiomolybdate, Yu et al. reported the novel synthesis of Ni–Co co-doped $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(Ni{-}C o{-}M o S_{2}}^{\prime}$ ) nanoboxes consisting of nanosheet assembled walls.[140] The Ni–Co PBA nanocubes undergo an unusual etching process originating from the different reactivity between the edges and the plane surfaces of PBA precursors (Figure 3l). At the same time, Ni–Co incorporated $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets are grown on the nanocube templates to form Ni–Co- ${\cdot\mathrm{MoS}_{2}}$ nanoboxes (Figure $3\mathrm{m-p}^{\cdot}$ ).
## 3.3. Multishelled Hollow Structures
Creating hollow particles with multiple shell architectures from MOFs could be achieved by innovative synthesis methods and/or delicate control over the shell formation process. As an example, Hu et al. have demonstrated a two-step method to prepare $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ double-shelled nanocages (DSNCs) with different shell compositions using simple MOF particles as precursors (Figure 4a).[44] In the first step, the as-prepared ZIF-67 particles are dispersed in $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ solution to produce ZIF $\therefore67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ LDH yolk–shelled structures. Afterward, annealing treatment is applied to further convert the ZIF-67 cores and Ni–Co LDH shells into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nano­ cages, respectively. Both the inner $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ layer and the outer $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ layer remain intact after thermal treatment, giving rise to the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs (Figure 4b,c). In a follow-up study, Hu et al. reported another multistep approach to synthesize Co/carbon $@{\mathrm{Co}}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ DSNCs.[131] The ZIF-67 templates are initially dispersed in a thioacetamide solution at elevated temperature to form ZIF-67@CoS yolk–shelled structures by the ion exchange reaction between ZIF-67 and $S^{2-}$ ions released by the decomposition of thioacetamide. Afterward, thermal carbonization treatment is applied to transform the amorphous CoS shell to $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ in nitrogen atmosphere. Meanwhile, the ZIF-67 cores inside the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ shells are converted to Co nanoparticle-embedded carbon nanocages (Figure 4d,e).
Besides these multistep approaches, highly complex particles with multiple layers of shells can also be fabricated from unique CP templates in one-step synthesis. Guan et al. first demonstrated the synthesis of highly complex seven-layered Ni–Co oxide hollow particles via calcination of Ni–Co-based CP precursors (Figure 4f).[63] Uniform Ni–Co CP precursors are initially prepared from coprecipitation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions, and isophthalic acid. These CP spheres are easily oxidized to metal oxide under annealing treatment in air, while the in situ formed Ni–Co oxide shell can spall off on its own very fast, to generate multishelled hollow structures up to seven thin layers (Figure $4\mathrm{g-j}.$ ). More importantly, these unique amorphous CPs can incorporate many other metal ions, making it a universal method for multishelled hollow particles with tunable compositions. In addition, combining with a sequential ion exchange approach, $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ multishelled particles with hollow structured shells have been successfully prepared (Figure 4k).[129] Sevenlayered $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles are first fabricated by thermal treatment of a Co-based CP template. Then, an anion exchange reaction is carried out between seven-layered $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles and $S^{2-}$ ions to form seven-layered $\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ particles with special hollow structured shells (Figure 4l). Afterward, these onion-like $\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ particles can further undergo cation exchange with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions to form $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ particles with well retained structure (Figure $4\mathrm{m}^{\cdot}$ ).
Formation of multishelled structures with hierarchical shells composed of 2D nanosheet subunits could be achieved from MOF-based precursors. As a typical example, ZIF-67 rhombic dodecahedra have been used to prepare novel double-shelled nanocages consisting of cobalt hydroxide (CH) inner shells and Ni–Co layered double hydroxides (LDHs) outer shells through two individual conversion reactions (Figure 5a).[141] Specifically, ZIF-67 templates are converted into yolk–shelled $\underline{{{7}}}\mathrm{IF}{-}67\ @\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ LDH particles with rough outer shells constructed by small nanosheets via chemical reaction with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ . The ZIF-67 cores in these yolk–shelled particles further react with $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in an alkaline solution of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}$ to produce the double-shelled $\mathrm{CH}@\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{Co}$ LDH particles (Figure 5b). Moreover, PB nanocubes have been utilized as templates to prepare highly complex nanoboxes consisting of nanosheet subunits through an anion exchange reaction with $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in NaOH solution.[45] Hierarchical ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ boxes with one to four shells can be prepared under hydrothermal conditions by increasing the inward diffusion of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ and the precipitation rate (Figure 5c–f).
Unlike most multishelled hierarchical hollow structures composed of one type of primary building blocks, e.g., nanosheets in above-mentioned studies, Hu et al. first fabri­ cated a unique multishelled architecture with both nano­ particle and nanosheet building blocks (Figure 5g).[130] ZIF-67/ ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ yolk–shelled structures are first prepared by refluxing ZIF-67 nanocubes (Figure 5h) in a mixed solvent of ethanol and water. Figure 5i shows the TEM image of ZIF-67/ ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ yolk–shelled structures composed of solid ZIF-67 cores and ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanosheet shells. Then, a direct sulfidation treatment of ZIF-67/ ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ yolk–shelled particles is carried out to produce unique double-shelled hollow particles with CoS-nanoparticle assembled nanoboxes surrounded by CoS-nanosheetconstructed shells (CoS-NP/CoS-NS DSNBs, Figure 5j,k).
## 3.4. Other Unusual Complex Structures
Apart from the conventional porous or/and hollow architectures, unusual nanostructures have also been reported starting from MOF-based precursors. For example, unique frame-like NiS nano­ particles can be synthesized from Ni–Co-based PBA through an anion exchange induced anisotropic chemical etching approach in $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ solution.[128] The structural evolution process from Ni–Co PBA nanocubes to NiS nanoframes is illustrated in Figure 6a. The etching process starts with the edges of Ni–Co PBA precursor (Figure 6b), leaving a NiS skeleton (Figure 6c). After prolonged reaction, the six plane surfaces of the intermediate are gradually dissolved to produce hollow space (Figure 6d). Finally, a frame-like nanostructure constructed by small nano­ particle subunits is formed (Figure 6e). Furthermore, the different reactivity between edge and surface of these Ni–Co PBA nanocubes also leads to the formation of complex PBA nanocages composed of pyramid-like walls through a controlled chemical etching process in ammonia solution.[71] The inhomogeneous bulk reactivity of cubic Ni–Co PBA precursors promotes fast etching along the body diagonal direction to remove the central defect-rich regions. The obtained singlecrystalline Ni–Co PBA nanocages can be further transformed to porous Ni–Co oxide nanocages assembled by six identical pyramid-like walls via a subsequent thermal treatment in air (Figure 6f,g).
Alternatively, manipulating the architecture of MOF-based precursors would also allow the fabrication of unusual nanostructures that could not be obtained from solid MOF particles. As an example, Guan et al. reported an MOF-composite assisted synthesis of single-holed cobalt/N-doped carbon $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{C}_{0}/$ NC) hollow particles (Figure 6h,i).[142] A uniform layer of ZIF-67 particles is first grown on polystyrene (PS) spheres to form PS $@$ ZIF-67 composite particles as the precursors. These core– shelled MOF composites are further transformed into hollow particles with a large through-hole on the surface through a carbonization process. The formation of the hole on the surface of Co/NC hollow particle is due to the strong gas outflux of hydrocarbon generated from the decomposition of PS sphere. In addition, novel CoS cages with unusual bubble-like subunits have been prepared through a “nanoparticles-in-MOF” dual template strategy (Figure 6j,k).[143] This approach involves encapsulation of mesostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /hexadecylamine $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\right.$ HDA) nanospheres as co-templates in each ZIF-67 polyhedron through a blending assembly process, followed by transformation of this hybrid precursor into hierarchical cage with CoS nanobubble subunits in the shell through a sulfidation/ etching process. The introduction of degradable $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{HDA}$ nanospheres into each ZIF-67 crystal provides new interfaces between the MOF host and co-templates, which favor the formation of CoS moieties to produce bubble-like subunits during the sulfidation process.
Furthermore, hierarchical hollow structures have been synthesized by delicately controlled multistep approaches. For example, Yu et al. developed a sequential diffusion-controlled approach to prepare hierarchical hollow prisms consisting of nanosized $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ bubble-shaped building blocks (Figure $^{61,\mathrm{m}}$ ).[66] ZIF-67 hollow prisms assembled by interconnected nano-polyhedra are first synthesized. These polyhedral subunits are further transformed into $\mathrm{Co}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ bubble-shaped hollow particles to produce the complex hollow prisms via a sulfidation treatment. Moreover, hierarchical tubes composed of both hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and CNT subunits have been prepared through a multistep route (Figure 6n).[144] Starting from cobalt acetate/polymer composite fibers, core–shelled cobalt acetate/polymer $@$ ZIF-67 composite fibers are initially prepared via partial phase transformation reaction with organic ligand 2-methylimidazole. After the selective dissolution of cobalt acetate/polymer cores, the as-obtained ZIF-67 tubes are pyrolyzed under argon/ hydrogen atmosphere to generate hierarchical tubes consisting of CNTs and Co/carbon composites in the shells. Afterward, a secondary heat treatment in air removes amorphous carbon residue and produces hierarchical $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C N T}$ tubes.
## 4. Complex MOF-Derived Nanostructures for Energy-Related Applications
## 4.1. Lithium-Ion Batteries
LIBs with high energy density, no memory effect, and low self-discharge are the dominant rechargeable battery for portable electronics,[145–147] and under intensive study as promising energy sources for electric cars and smart grid systems.[148–150] Nevertheless, large-scale applications of LIBs ask for a comprehensive upgrade of battery systems to provide improved safety, high energy/power density, and prolonged cycle life. A typical LIB is composed of an anode and a cathode, separated by an insulating porous membrane saturated with liquid electrolyte.[151] The properties of electrode materials play the key role on the electrochemical performance of LIBs.[18] To seek for advanced materials to replace commercial graphite anode, various high-capacity candidates, including transition metal oxides and sulfides, have been intensively investigated.[10,152,153] Nevertheless, the application of these materials has been hindered by the rapid capacity fading associated with the large volume change during lithiation/delithiation processes and the poor rate capability due to the low diffusion rate of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions within electrode materials.
Design and preparation of proper nanostructured electrode materials have been considered with great potential to overcome these issues. MOF-derived porous/hollow nanostructures would allow efficient lithium storage capability owing to enlarged exposed surface, reduced ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ ion diffusion distance and void space to buffer mechanical stress; incorporation of carbonaceous components would further promote electron conduction and improve structural robustness. As an example, guided by an MOF-engaged self-templating strategy, CoSe/C nanoboxes with complex structured shells have been prepared, in which a CoSe-rich inner layer is confined within a carbon-rich outer layer (Figure 3c,d and Figure  7a,b).[65] When evaluated as an electrode material for LIBs, the CoSe/C nanoboxes deliver exceptional rate performance with capacity retention as high as $87\%$ with a tenfold increase of current density (Figure 7c). The porous carbon framework increases the conductivity of the electrode materials and provides pathways for charge transport, thus permitting the efficient lithiation/delithiation at high current densities. Chen et al. investigated the lithium storage property of a hierarchical tubular nanostructure constructed by hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticle and CNT subunits (Figure 7d).[144] These hierarchical $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C N T}$ tubes afford excellent rate performance (Figure  7e) with average specific capacities of
832, 786, 715, 673, and $643\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}}$ at current densities of 0.75, 1.25, 2, 2.5, and $3\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Additionally, remarkable cycling stability is shown at high rates (1 and $4\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})$ without obvious capacity fading for over 200 cycles (Figure 7f). The hierarchical nanostructure may effectively buffer the volume variation during electrochemical processes, leading to the improved cycling performance.
The morphology of structural subunits plays a crucial role on lithium storage performance. Zhang et al. have investigated the effect of secondary subunits of hierarchical hollow structures on electrochemical behavior in LIBs. ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes composed of different structural subunits, such as nanoparticles and nanosheets, have been prepared by calcination of PB cubes at different temperatures.[139] As shown in Figure 3h,i and $7\mathrm{g-i}.$ these ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes possess different building blocks in terms of size and shape. The hierarchical ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes consisting of nanosheet-like subunits (Figure 3h,i and 7i) exhibit a high specific capacity of $945~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 30 cycles (Figure 7j). However, ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes (Figure $\mathrm{7g)}$ and porous ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ boxes (Figure 7h) constructed by ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ nanoparticles with different sizes, show lower specific capacities of 802 and $871\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ , respectively. Furthermore, integration of different components in hollow nanostructures may render them synergistic advantages, which promise enhancement in lithium storage properties. Such a synergistic effect has been exampled by novel $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3^{-}}$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ composite boxes via delicately designed PB-engaged reactions.[45] The elemental mappings in Figure $^{7\mathrm{k}}$ reveal an even distribution of Fe, Sn, and O elements throughout the whole box. Of note, these $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ composite boxes exhibit higher specific capacity and cycling stability compared with pure $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ boxes (Figure $^{71,\mathrm{m}}$ ).
Besides the intensive research in LIBs, MOF-derived materials have also been applied as electrode materials for other novel rechargeable battery systems, such as sodium-ion and lithium–sulfur batteries.[154] However, use of complex nanostructures from MOF-based precursors for these electrochemical systems is rarely reported so far. In view of the unique structural and compositional advantages, we anticipate that MOF-derived complex nanomaterials would hold great potential as electrode materials for high-performance rechargeable batteries beyond LIBs.
## 4.2. Hybrid Supercapacitors
HSCs are a type of novel energy storage system, bridging the gap between high-energy batteries and high-power supercapacitors.[52,155–157] HSCs consist of a high-rate battery-type electrode, such as metal oxides and sulfides, providing high energy density; the other electrode is usually composed of porous carbon material with double-layer capacitance to offer high power output.[52,158] While HSCs can potentially achieve both high energy and power densities, their practical applications are hindered by the sluggish kinetics and insufficient durability of the battery-type electrode.
The kinetics and durability of conventional battery-type electrode materials could be improved using complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based precursors, similar to the cases of LIBs. As an example, Yu et al. reported a special framelike NiS nanoparticle derived from MOF template (Figure 6a–e and Figure 8a,b).[128] As a battery-type electrode material for HSCs in alkaline media, these nanoframes exhibit a high specific capacitance of $2112\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at the current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 8c), superior to many previous reported sulfidebased materials.[129,159,160] Additionally, the excellent cycling capability of NiS nanoframes with only $8\%$ capacitance loss over 4000 cycles is shown in Figure 8d. Moreover, the energy storage capability could be further boosted by increasing the surface area of hollow structured electrode materials with high complexity. Guan and Lou designed novel CoS polyhedral cages consisting of nanobubble subunits in the shells as a batterytype electrode material for HSCs (Figure 6j,k and 8e,f).[143] Due to high surface area and rich active sites, the CoS nanobubble cages can store larger amount of charges than simple CoS cages (Figure 8g). About $72\%$ of the capacitance for CoS nanobubble cages is retained when the current density increases from 1 to $20\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , revealing excellent rate capability (Figure 8h).
Multishelled hollow structures can increase the energy storage performance by maximizing exposed surface and optimizing void space, and extend the cycle life by improving structural stability. In a related work, hierarchical doubleshelled hollow structures with CoS nanoparticle-constructed nanoboxes surrounded by CoS-nanosheet-assembled shell (CoS-NP/CoS-NS DSNBs, Figure $^{5\mathrm{j,k}}$ ) are controllably synthesized and evaluated as a battery-type electrode material for HSCs.[130] Compared with two types of single-shelled nanoboxes composed of CoS nanoparticles (CoS-NP SSNBs) and CoS nanosheets (CoS-NS SSNBs), the CoS-NP/CoS-NS DSNBs afford superior rate performance (Figure 8i) with high capacitance of 980, 920, 865, 749, and $585~\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}$ at current densities of 1, 2, 5, 10, and $20\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Furthermore, compared with their single-shelled counterparts, long-term cycling test also reveals improved electrochemical stability of the double-shelled CoS-NP/CoS-NS DSNBs for over $10\ 000$ cycles (Figure  8j). Guan et al. have recently demonstrated an HSC device using seven-shelled $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hollow spheres as the battery-type electrode (Figure $4\mathrm{f-j\uparrow}$ ),[63] and graphene/multishelled mesoporous carbon spheres $(\mathrm{G/MMCSs})$ composites as the capacitive electrode in $6\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution (Figure 8k). As shown in Figure 8l, the HSC device shows remarkable cycling stability with $91.3\%$ of the initial capacitance retained after 10 000 cycles. Additionally, the HSC device exhibits a high energy density of $52.6~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ at power density of $1604~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ (Figure $8\mathrm m)$ ), which is superior to many previously reported HSC devices.[159,161–163] These studies highlight the advantages of multishelled hollow structures and their structure-dependent electrochemical behaviors.
## 4.3. Electrocatalysis
Sustainable and environmentally friendly energy-conversion technologies, including water splitting electrolyzers and fuel cells, are essential for future low-carbon-emission economy.[138,164] These systems depend on several important electrochemical reactions including the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR).[165–167] However, the efficiency of these electrochemical reactions is severely limited by the sluggish reaction kinetics, which require highly efficient catalysts to overcome energy barrier at relatively low overpotentials. Porous and/or hollow nanostructures with high surface area and open pore structure can provide abundant easily accessible active sites and facile mass/charge transport, which are highly beneficial for electrocatalytic reactions.
Recently, Wu et al. have designed novel MOF-derived mesoporous $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ octahedra as electrocatalysts for HER in both acidic and alkaline solutions (Figure $2\mathrm{f-h}$ and Figure 9a,b).[67]
The $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ electrocatalyst shows an excellent onset potential of $25~\mathrm{mV}$ in 0.5 m ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution, and small overpotentials of 87 and $142\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve current densities of 1.0 and $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (Figure 9c), respectively. The $\mathrm{MoC}_{x}$ catalyst exhibits similar high activity in alkaline electrolyte with an onset potential of $80~\mathrm{mV},$ as well as overpotentials of 92 and $151~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach current densities of 1.0 and $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively (Figure 9d). Additionally, introduction of heterometal ions into catalysts might offer additional active sites and/or modify electronic structures of catalysts to lower energy barriers of electrochemical reactions. Ni–Co co-incorporated $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{Co}{\cdot}\mathrm{MoS}_{2})$ nanoboxes consisting of ultrathin nanosheets are synthesized via a MOFengaged self-templated strategy (Figure $3\mathrm{l-p}$ and 9e,f).[140] As an electrocatalyst for HER in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution, these nanoboxes exhibit lower onset overpotential of $125~\mathrm{mV},$ compared with bare $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets (Figure $|9\mathrm{g}\rangle$ . Tafel plots demonstrate the improved electrocatalytic activity of ${\mathrm{Ni}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Co}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}}$ nanoboxes with a small Tafel slope of $51\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ (Figure 9h).
Similarly, the electrocatalytic performance of MOF-derived catalysts for OER can be improved by controlling their architectures and compositions. The Ni–Co oxide nanocages constructed by six pyramidal walls (Figure 6f,g and 9i,j) preserve the morphology of their parent Ni–Co PBA precursors.[71] These open structured nanocages show much higher current density toward OER at a fixed overpotential of $400~\mathrm{mV}$ compared with Ni–Co oxide nanocubes in alkaline solution (Figure 9k). Guan et al. evaluated the OER performance of multishelled $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn-Co}$ oxyphosphide particles (Figure $^{\mathrm{9l,m}}$ ) in alkaline media.[64] After the constant-potential activation process, abundant oxide/ hydroxide species are formed in Mn–Co oxyphosphide nanoshells. Compared with pristine Mn–Co oxyphosphide and Mn–Co oxide particles, these activated particles manifest the lowest overpotential of $320~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of
$10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 9n). Moreover, the small Tafel slope of $52\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ for the activated Mn–Co oxyphosphide sample verifies the excellent electrocatalytic kinetics (Figure 9o).
Apart from two electrocatalytic water splitting reactions, the advantage of MOF-derived nanostructures is further confirmed by the remarkable ORR performance of hollow frameworks constructed by NCNTFs.[138] As shown in FESEM and TEM images (Figure 3e,f and 9p,q), the NCNTFs consisting of interconnected CNT building blocks in the shell possess a large cavity. From the polarization curve in Figure 9r, a high half-wave potential of 0.87 V versus reversible hydrogen electrode is observed, demonstrating the excellent electrocatalytic activity for ORR. Compared with a commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst, the NCNTFs exhibit superior electrochemical stability for longterm operation (Figure 9s).
## 5. Conclusions and Outlook
The rapidly expanding family of MOFs has promised widespread applications in energy- and environment-related fields. While the direct adaptation of MOFs for electrochemical devices is often limited by their insufficient thermal/chemical stability and low electrical conductivity, metal/carbon-based nanostructures derived from MOF-based materials have shown outstanding electrochemical properties. Of particular note is the capability of MOF-based precursors to fabricate complex nanostructures. These complex nanostructures might possess exceptional merits originated from their multiple compositional or structural features, resulting in superior performance for electrochemical applications.
Construction of complex nanostructures from MOF-based precursors could be primarily controlled by manipulating the precursors and/or the conversion process to various functional nanomaterials. Despite great advances achieved in recent years, most of the studies are limited to a few well-investigated MOFs, such as PBAs and ZIFs. From a practical point of view, it would be desirable to use MOF-based composite precursors composed of low cost and easily synthesized MOFs together with other components to bring additional functionalities (Figure  10). Such composite precursors could be prepared by encapsulating co-precursors in pore channels of MOFs or co­assembly of MOFs with other components. Such strategies would greatly broaden the availability of MOF-based precursors, and thus lead to successful synthesis of complex nanostructures with better controlled functionalities. For conversion processes from MOF-based precursors to functional materials, improved solution- or annealing-based methods with better controllability and scalability, as well as novel synthetic approaches are highly desirable.
Morphology and composition are two primary considerations when synthesizing MOF-derived complex nanostructures with specific functionalities. However, most of the MOFderived nanostructures are composed of particles with high isotropic shape. Other morphologies, including 1D/2D, hierarchical and 3D self-supported architectures are less reported, which are limited by the available synthetic methods. On the other hand, metal oxide, sulfide, carbon, and their composite nanostructures from MOF-based templates have been widely reported, whereas other metal-based components such as carbides, pnictides, and chalcogenides are less explored. Incorporation of nonmetal elements, such as B, N, F, P, and S, can effectively alter the intrinsic activity of MOF-derived carbon materials by tuning their electronic structures, which remains mostly unexplored. All these challenges might be overcome by making novel MOF-based composite precursors and innovating their conversion methods toward complex functional nanostructures.
In the past few years, the rapid development of complex nanostructures from MOF-based materials has much benefited the development of many electrochemical energy storage and conversion technologies. However, the fundamental relationships between complex nanostructures and their exceptional electrochemical properties are still waiting for in-depth investigations. In addition to experimental studies, theoretical calculation and modeling, which would provide important insights for understanding the relation between complex nanostructures and their electrochemical performances, are still rare. There has been some progress on designed synthesis of MOF-derived functional materials with targeted functionalities. Despite these advances, challenges remain in establishing universal synthetic approaches and well-defined structure-/composition-induced properties. With continuous efforts devoted, we optimistically anticipate that exceptional complex nanostructures derived from MOF-based materials would bring breakthrough to the vast field of energy- and environment-related applications.
